 ## Overview
-This method synthesizes a 3D volumetric texture (specifically cloud volumes) from a single input photograph by minimizing an energy function that measures the distance between the input image pixel blocks and the rendered voxel blocks [S1]. It adapts the patch-based texture synthesis technique of Kwatra et al. to the 3D volume domain, treating voxel blocks as the fundamental units of synthesis [S1]. Unlike standard volume rendering which uses fixed parameters, this method optimizes the voxel values themselves to match visual evidence [S1]. It is distinguished from its parent [Dual Rendering Equation Optimization](8167-dual-rendering-equation-optimization.md) by explicitly solving for the volume geometry and appearance via an Expectation-Maximization (EM) framework rather than optimizing only lighting parameters [S1].
+This method synthesizes a 3D volumetric texture (specifically cloud volumes) from a single input photograph by minimizing an energy function that measures the distance between the input image pixel blocks and the rendered voxel blocks [S1]. It adapts the patch-based texture synthesis technique of Kwatra et al. to the 3D volume domain, treating voxel blocks as the fundamental units of synthesis [S1]. Unlike standard volume rendering which uses fixed parameters, this method optimizes the voxel values themselves to match visual evidence [S1]. It is distinguished from its parent [Dual Rendering Equation Optimization](8167-dual-rendering-equation-optimization.md) by explicitly solving for the volume geometry/appearance via an Expectation-Maximization (EM) framework rather than optimizing only lighting parameters [S1].
 
